.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A session: the transport, user authentication and the connection protocol joined into one object with one set of events.
.PP
It is a facade over conn.h, auth.h and chan.h, which stay public and usable on their own (a proxy that wants to route messages differently can).  What the session does for the application is the plumbing that every application would otherwise write the same way:
.IP \(bu 3
routes messages 50 to 79 to the authentication machine and 80 to 100 to the channels, and ends the connection if a peer sends a channel message before authentication, or a message number nobody knows (that one is answered with SSH_MSG_UNIMPLEMENTED);
.IP \(bu 3
requests the ssh\-userauth service, hands the authentication machine the session identifier and the host key, and passes it the server's extensions (RFC 8308), including server\-sig\-algs and the host\-bound public key method;
.IP \(bu 3
turns every layer's request to end the connection into a DISCONNECT;
.IP \(bu 3
starts key exchanges when due, and resumes channels that were held back by one;
.IP \(bu 3
keeps one deadline for the application to wait for, the earliest of the transport's and the authentication grace time;
.IP \(bu 3
enforces what a user's certificate permits, or the application takes away (an authorized_keys line, authkeys.h): no pty without permit\-pty, no forwarding without permit\-port\-forwarding;
.IP \(bu 3
enforces no\-more\-sessions@openssh.com and a limit on session channels, and refuses what the application has not been asked to decide: with no channel_open callback every channel is refused, and with no global_request callback every global request is.
.PP
Everything is optional except the write callback, the host key callback of a client, and the authentication request callback of a server.  A client with no host_key callback trusts nothing.
.PP
SFTP is bound to a channel with gf_session_sftp_server or gf_session_sftp_client; the session then carries its data, flushes its output when the channel can take more, and closes the channel if the SFTP session fails.
.PP
The session has the phases HANDSHAKE (key exchange), AUTH and READY (authenticated; channels allowed), then CLOSED.
.PP
Time is the caller's clock in microseconds, as everywhere.  Calls that take a time update the session's idea of it; the channel functions do not take one, and use the time of the last call that did (or of gf_session_set_time).  Threading: one session is used from one thread at a time, and callbacks must not free it.
.SH "TYPES AND CONSTANTS"
.PP
.nf
enum gf_session_phase
{
  GF_PHASE_HANDSHAKE = 1,
  GF_PHASE_AUTH,
  GF_PHASE_READY,
  GF_PHASE_CLOSED
};
