const char *\fBgf_session_force_command\fP (const gf_session *s);
A server, after authentication: the command the user's certificate forces ("force\-command") or the application forced (gf_session_force), or NULL if there is none.  If there is one the application must run it, and only it, in place of whatever the client asks for in an exec, shell or subsystem request.  Valid until the session is freed.
.TP
int \fBgf_session_permits\fP (const gf_session *s, const char *permission);
A server: does the user's certificate permit PERMISSION (one of the GF_CERT_PERMIT_* names of key.h)?  1 for a user who did not use a certificate, who is not restricted by one.  The session itself refuses a pty request without "permit\-pty", and a direct\-tcpip or direct\-streamlocal channel or a tcpip\-forward or streamlocal\-forward request without "permit\-port\-forwarding", before the application is asked; agent and X11 forwarding are never offered.  The rest, "permit\-user\-rc" (whether to run ~/.ssh/rc) among them, is the application's to ask here.
.TP
int \fBgf_session_deny\fP (gf_session *s, const char *permission);
A server: take PERMISSION (one of the GF_CERT_PERMIT_* names of key.h) away from the user, whatever their certificate says, for the pty request, direct\-tcpip and direct\-streamlocal channels and tcpip\-forward and streamlocal\-forward requests the session refuses itself and for gf_session_permits.  For what an authorized_keys line says (authkeys.h): call it for each permission gf_authkey_permits denies. GF_ERR_INVAL for a name that is not one of the five, or for a client.
.TP
int \fBgf_session_force\fP (gf_session *s, const char *command);
A server: the user must be given COMMAND and nothing else (gf_session_force_command), as an authorized_keys line "command=" says. GF_ERR_INVAL if the user's certificate or an earlier call forces another command: a user two things say different things about is to be refused, which is for the application to do.
.TP
gf_chans *\fBgf_session_channels\fP (gf_session *s);
.br
gf_conn *\fBgf_session_conn\fP (gf_session *s);
The layers underneath, for what the session does not wrap: channel operations (open, send data, requests, close: see chan.h) and transport operations (rekey, debug, ignore).
